<body>
    <div id="site-header"></div>

    <!-- Hero -->
    <div class="page-hero">
        <h1>Our Mission</h1>
        <div class="divider"></div>
        <p class="subtitle">
            Bringing augmented reality navigation indoors — making every building as easy to navigate as an open map.
        </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Mission Statement -->
    <div style="max-width:900px; margin:0 auto; padding:0 16px 0;">
        <div class="mission-statement">
            <blockquote>
                "To develop an accessible, real-time augmented reality indoor navigation system that empowers students,
                faculty, and visitors at Florida Tech to find their way through complex indoor environments
                with confidence and ease."
            </blockquote>
            <cite>— FITARNA Senior Design Team, Florida Institute of Technology</cite>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Problem Statement -->
    <div class="section">
        <h2>The Problem</h2>
        <div class="problem-body">
            <p>
                GPS technology has transformed outdoor navigation, but it fails entirely the moment you step inside
                a building. Multi-story academic buildings, labs, and event centers can be genuinely disorienting —
                especially for new students, guests, or anyone with accessibility needs.
            </p>
            <p>
                Traditional solutions like printed maps and static directory boards are easy to miss, hard to update,
                and provide no turn-by-turn guidance. FITARNA addresses this gap by using the camera already in
                every smartphone to overlay live navigation cues directly onto the real world.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Goals -->
    <div class="section">
        <h2>Project Goals</h2>
        <div class="goal-grid">
            <div class="goal-card">
                <div class="goal-icon">📍</div>
                <h3>Real-Time Positioning</h3>
                <p>Use AR Foundation and on-device sensors to accurately determine a user's location and orientation within a mapped building.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="goal-card">
                <div class="goal-icon">🗺️</div>
                <h3>Turn-by-Turn Guidance</h3>
                <p>Overlay directional waypoints and route highlights onto the camera feed, guiding users to any room or point of interest.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="goal-card">
                <div class="goal-icon">📱</div>
                <h3>Cross-Platform Support</h3>
                <p>Deliver a seamless experience on both iOS (ARKit) and Android (ARCore) devices through a shared Unity codebase.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="goal-card">
                <div class="goal-icon">♿</div>
                <h3>Accessibility First</h3>
                <p>Design navigation paths and UI elements with accessibility in mind, supporting users of all abilities.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="goal-card">
                <div class="goal-icon">⚡</div>
                <h3>Low-Latency Experience</h3>
                <p>Ensure AR overlays remain stable and responsive even in challenging lighting conditions typical of indoor spaces.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="goal-card">
                <div class="goal-icon">🏫</div>
                <h3>Campus Integration</h3>
                <p>Map Florida Tech buildings with accurate floor plans so the system can be deployed and extended to new buildings over time.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Technology -->
    <div class="section">
        <h2>How We Get There</h2>
        <div class="problem-body">
            <p>
                FITARNA is built on Unity's AR Foundation framework, which provides a unified abstraction layer over
                ARCore (Android) and ARKit (iOS). This lets the team write shared C# logic while still leveraging
                each platform's native AR capabilities.
            </p>
            <p>
                Floor-plan data is served via a TypeScript backend, which the app queries at runtime to load the
                correct map for the building being navigated. A pathfinding layer computes the optimal route and
                converts it into a series of 3D waypoints that are anchored to real-world coordinates and rendered
                as AR overlays.
            </p>
            <div class="badge-row">
                <span class="badge">Unity</span>
                <span class="badge">AR Foundation</span>
                <span class="badge">ARCore</span>
                <span class="badge">ARKit</span>
                <span class="badge">C#</span>
                <span class="badge">TypeScript</span>
                <span class="badge">URP</span>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <script type="module" src="/src/theme.ts"></script>
    <script type="module" src="/src/navbar.ts"></script>

    <noscript>
    <div style="padding:12px; text-align:center; background:#fee; border-top:1px solid #fbb;">
